#dipping uncursed water into holy water to make holy water makes sense
to me (HW blesses anything it touches, including other water). Same
logic applies to dipping uncursed water into unholy water to make
unholy water.

#praying with water on an altar to make holy water makes sense to me
(my god blesses my water).

#praying with water on a differently-aligned altar to make unholy
water makes sense to me (it angers my god, who curses my water), but I
don't want to lose protection and anger my god in the process.

Blessing a scroll of remove curse, #naming all my water potions
individually, dropping everything else in my inventory, confusing
myself, and then reading a ?oRC in order to randomly bless/curse/leave
alone each individual potion of water is not intuitive to me. It
doesn't seem to me that a character would ever think of doing that.

It's not that I would never do it, if I were in a spot; it's just that
I tend to roleplay a bit more than most players, I think, and I don't
like doing things that are counterintuitive to me. In this game, it
was my own bloody fault for using up the one potion of unholy water I
found. (This is how I usually get unholy water: find a potion--usually
in a shop--and then #dip to keep a supply going.)
